      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "My apologies for the confusion. Let's dive straight into the technical aspects of your DNS propagation issue. When you update an A record, the change needs to propagate across the internet's DNS servers. This process can take time, and the inconsistency you're experiencing could be due to several factors:\n\n1. **TTL (Time to Live) Values**: The TTL value in your DNS records dictates how long a DNS server should cache the record before checking for updates. If the TTL is set high, it may take longer for all servers to receive the new IP address. Lowering the TTL temporarily before making changes can speed up propagation.\n\n2. **DNS Server Caching**: Different DNS servers around the world may have different caching policies. Some may cache the old record longer than others, leading to inconsistencies in which users are directed to the old or new IP.\n\n3. **Propagation Delays**: Due to the distributed nature of the DNS system, changes can take anywhere from a few minutes to 48 hours to fully propagate. During this time, some users may be directed to the old IP while others see the new one.\n\n4. **DNS Resolver Behavior**: The DNS resolver your users are querying might be using a different set of DNS servers",
